{"root":{"data":{"id":"de294020029e","created":1666634070,"text":"Cloud Computing & Networking"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cnuciquo9140","created":1666634211253,"text":"DevOps"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cnucj9sg1bc0","created":1666634252477,"text":"Background","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cnuck6d061c0","created":1666634323377,"text":"Business needs"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"cnuckefuqv40","created":1666634340964,"text":"Meaning"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cnucku5y74w0","created":1666634375194,"text":"Development+Operations"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cnucky9uj0w0","created":1666634384136,"text":"improved collaboration, communication, and integration "},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"cnucnodkgio0","created":1666634597686,"text":"Infrastructure as Code","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cnuco6yvleo0","created":1666634638157,"text":"traditionally manual process"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cnucpivri740","created":1666634742454,"text":"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\ttreat infrastructure == treat code \n\t\t"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"cnucq707e6w0","created":1666634794965,"text":"Automation"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cnucqdaj3qg0","created":1666634808650,"text":"focuses on the setup, configuration, deployment, and support of infrastucture and the applications that run on it"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cnucrwsvcuo0","created":1666634929483,"text":"standardized and repeatable "},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cnucrye74880","created":1666634932949,"text":"remove manual process"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cnucsbtqofc0","created":1666634962187,"text":"Benefits","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cnuct93h8f40","created":1666635034609,"text":"• Rapid Changes\n• Improved Productivity\n• Repeatable Configurations \n• Leveraged Elasticity\n• Leveraged auto scaling\n• Automated Test"},"children":[]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"cnuctid3puo0","created":1666635054782,"text":"Monitoring"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cnuctyuavo00","created":1666635090651,"text":"\t\t"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cnucugji3xc0","created":1666635129180,"text":"logs,"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cnucugjipg00","created":1666635129181,"text":"monitoring,"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cnucugjip3c0","created":1666635129181,"text":"alerting and auditing"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cnucugjiyiw0","created":1666635129181,"text":"infrastructure "},"children":[]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"cnucuopjiig0","created":1666635146959,"text":"Security"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"cnucv1bry200","created":1666635174425,"text":"Linux/Vi/Shell"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cnucvk37rps0","created":1666635215266,"text":"Linux","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cnucwmj00c80","created":1666635298938,"text":"broad: the entire operating system\nexact: the operating system kernel"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cnudg4gr39c0","created":1666636826904,"text":"UEFI"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"co91rj6jelk0","created":1668127266975,"text":"Unified Extensible Firmware Interface (UEFI) is a specification for a software program that connects a computer's firmware to its operating system (OS). \nUEFI is expected to eventually replace basic input/output system (BIOS) but is compatible with it.","font-weight":"bold"},"children":[]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"co91uiavwbk0","created":1668127500153,"text":"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tThe kernel assumes a role like that of a conductor in an orchestra—it ensures coordination between hardware and software.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tThis role includes managing hardware, processes, users, permissions, and the file system.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tThe kernel provides a common base to all other programs on the system and typically runs in ring zero, also known as kernel space. \n\t\t"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"co91z9iw9g80","created":1668127872868,"text":"Components","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"co91znp8l8g0","created":1668127903726,"text":"• The Bootloader: The software that manages the boot process of your computer.\n• The kernel: This is the one piece of the whole that is actually called “Linux”. The kernel is the core of the system and manages the CPU, memory, and peripheral devices. The kernel is the “lowest” level of the OS.\n• Daemons: These are background services (printing, sound, scheduling, etc) that either start up during boot, or after you log into the desktop.\n• Desktop Environment: This is the piece of the puzzle that the users interact with.\n• Applications: Desktop environments do not offer the full array of apps.\n• The Shell: A command process that allows you to control the computer via\ncommands typed into a text interface."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"co9201lf1sg0","created":1668127933970,"text":"command line（Linux Shell）","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"co92675juzs0","created":1668128416256,"text":"Shell: An interface between the user and the kernel"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"co946g7p77c0","created":1668134078197,"text":"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t• sh - Bourne Shell\n• ksh - Korn Shell\n• csh / tcsh - C Shell\n• bash - Bourne-Again Shell\n• zsh - Z shell \n\t\t"},"children":[]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"cnucvmf4ib40","created":1666635220340,"text":"Vi"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cnucvnky5m00","created":1666635222869,"text":"Shell"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cov549844ig0","created":1670371046413,"text":"a shell script is a file containing a series of commands. "},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cov6kwtz9fs0","created":1670375172737,"text":"The shell reads this file and carries out the commands \nas though they have been entered directly on the command line.\t\t"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cov6m3b9y200","created":1670375265208,"text":"Shell scripts and functions are both interpreted. This means they are not compiled."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cov6mevvajc0","created":1670375290398,"text":" Scripts standardize and automate the performance of administrative chores \nand free up admins’ time for more important and more interesting tasks."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"cov6mz175zs0","created":1670375334256,"text":"linux characters","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cov6n4444bk0","created":1670375345316,"text":"Case Sensitivity \t\t"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cov6nddv7j40","created":1670375365497,"text":"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tSpecial Characters \n\t\t","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cov6ojuu18w0","created":1670375457948,"text":" All of the following characters have a special meaning or function. \nIf they are used in a way that their special meaning is not needed, they must be escaped.\n•\\/;,.~#$?&*()[]‘’\"+-!^=|<>"},"children":[]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"cov6qdjqvjs0","created":1670375600945,"text":"\"trap\" command"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cov6qj36k1c0","created":1670375613004,"text":"The trap command provides the script to captures an interrupt (signal) and then clean it up within the script."},"children":[]}]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"cov6r8weeqw0","created":1670375669190,"text":"Test/Git/CI/CD","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cov6zeu528g0","created":1670376309028,"text":"Tests","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cov6zknr19k0","created":1670376321702,"text":"Unit Test"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxml53ym6g0","created":1670623448427,"text":"fast&reliable"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxml7y7tv40","created":1670623454610,"text":"ØTake time to build \nØRequire maintenance"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxmojal8v40","created":1670623714395,"text":"Integration Test \t\t"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxmouuqljc0","created":1670623739558,"text":"Integration tests verify that interaction between multiple components (applications, services, modules, etc.) is working as expected."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxmpbgg6080","created":1670623775700,"text":"Performance/Load/Stress Testing "},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxmqssa7nk0","created":1670623891785,"text":" Test Impact Analysis (TIA) is a modern way of speeding up the test automation phase of a build."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxmr90fag00","created":1670623927105,"text":"GIT","expandState":"collapse"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxmrag3ifs0","created":1670623930230,"text":"version control"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxmrmyyfmg0","created":1670623957492,"text":"local version control"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxmrw3jsnc0","created":1670623977360,"text":"Revision Control System (RCS)\nSource Code Control System (SCCS)"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxms25ciyw0","created":1670623990530,"text":"Centralized Version Control"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxms7et6fc0","created":1670624001986,"text":"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t• CVS\n• SVN\n• Perforce \n\t\t"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxmsd825uo0","created":1670624014638,"text":"Distributed Version Control Systems"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxmsk990oo0","created":1670624029948,"text":"• Git\n• Mercurial • Bazaar\n• Darcs"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxmuqlhgao0","created":1670624200477,"text":"Git has integrity"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxmux428xs0","created":1670624214661,"text":"git lifecycle/status"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxmv6ivyz40","created":1670624235148,"text":"untracked"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxmvcb0evk0","created":1670624247733,"text":"unmodified"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxmvfdwby80","created":1670624254437,"text":"modified"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxmvh58jrk0","created":1670624258267,"text":"staged"},"children":[]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxmvz5cyfc0","created":1670624297457,"text":"Actions","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxmxjxz1j40","created":1670624421087,"text":"defi"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxmwbr7d3s0","created":1670624324899,"text":"GitHub Actions help you automate tasks within your software development life cycle.\nGitHub Actions are event-driven, meaning that you can run a series of commands after a specified event has occurred."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxmxmavzdc0","created":1670624426221,"text":"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tcomponents \n\t\t","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxmxmjs4co0","created":1670624426759,"text":"• Workflows "},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxmz2vyyw00","created":1670624540689,"text":"• The workflow is an automated procedure that you add to your repository.\n• Workflows are made up of one or more jobs and can be scheduled or triggered by an event.\n• The workflow can be used to build, test, package, release, or deploy a project on GitHub."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxmz9sc9uo0","created":1670624555707,"text":"• Events"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxmza4eo7k0","created":1670624556436,"text":"An event is a specific activity that triggers a workflow."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxmzpxkvhs0","created":1670624590852,"text":"• Jobs"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxmzqfc8o00","created":1670624591926,"text":"• A job is a set of steps that execute on the same runner.\n• By default, a workflow with multiple jobs will run those jobs in\nparallel."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxn015j79k0","created":1670624615278,"text":"Steps"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxn02tugh40","created":1670624618924,"text":"• A step is an individual task that can run commands in a job.\n• A step can be either an action or a shell command.\n\t\t"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxn0pw84l40","created":1670624669134,"text":"• Actions"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxn0zvh05c0","created":1670624690857,"text":"• Actions are standalone commands that are combined into steps to create a job.\n• Actions are the smallest portable building block of a workflow.\n• You can create your own actions, or use actions created by the GitHub\ncommunity.\n• To use an action in a workflow, you must include it as a step."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxmyfdftjs0","created":1670624489502,"text":"• Runners"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxn1a9x2000","created":1670624713498,"text":"• A runner is a server that has the GitHub Actions runner application installed.\n• A runner listens for available jobs, runs one job at a time, and reports\nthe progress, logs, and results back to GitHub."},"children":[]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxn23yyvts0","created":1670624778139,"text":"Encrypted Secrets"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxn26u14iw0","created":1670624784371,"text":"• Encrypted secrets allow you to store sensitive information in your organization, repository, or repository environments.\n• Secrets are encrypted environment variables that you create in an organization, repository, or repository environment."},"children":[]}]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxn77gmcgw0","created":1670625177558,"text":"CI/CD\nContinuous Integration, Delivery, & Deployment","expandState":"collapse"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxnb8hl1hk0","created":1670625493249,"text":"CI\nContinuous integration","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxngm1t5o00","created":1670625914591,"text":"defi","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxncktsw280","created":1670625598474,"text":"aim: prevent integration problems"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxncpx50u80","created":1670625609559,"text":"Continuous integration (CI) is the practice of merging all developer working copies to a shared mainline several times a day."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxnd4s366g0","created":1670625641906,"text":"CI is intended to be used in combination with automated unit tests "},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxng9j5o2g0","created":1670625887342,"text":"• In addition to automated unit tests, organizations using CI typically use a build server to implement continuous processes of applying quality control in general — small pieces of effort, applied frequently. \nIn addition to running the unit and integration tests, such processes run additional static and dynamic tests, measure and profile performance, extract and format documentation from the source code and facilitate manual QA processes.\n• This continuous application of quality control aims to improve the quality of software, and to reduce the time taken to deliver it, by replacing the traditional practice of applying quality control after completing all development."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxnhua761s0","created":1670626010877,"text":"Principles"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxnhuyv1m00","created":1670626012368,"text":"• Maintain a code repository\n• Automate the build\n• Make the build self-testing\n• Every commit (to baseline) should be built \n• Keep the build fast\n• Test in a clone of the production environment \n• Make it easy to get the latest deliverables\n• Everyone can see the results of the latest build \n• Automate deployment \n\t\t"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxnyj919880","created":1670627319053,"text":"Benefits"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxnyla6ha00","created":1670627323476,"text":"• Bugs are detected early\n• Avoids last-minute chaos at release dates\n• When unit tests fail or a bug emerges, if developers need to revert the codebase to a bug-free state without debugging, \nonly a small number of changes are lost (because integration happens frequently).\n• Constant availability of a \"current\" build for testing, demo, or release purposes\n• Frequent code check-in pushes developers to create modular, less complex code"},"children":[]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxnyujsjxc0","created":1670627343648,"text":"CD\nContinuous Deployment"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxo08wavi80","created":1670627453243,"text":"defi"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxo0aize5c0","created":1670627456792,"text":"• CD deployment: every code change goes through the entire pipeline and is put into production, automatically, resulting in many production deployments every day.\n• With Continuous Delivery your software is always release-ready, yet the timing of when to push it into production is a business decision, and so the final deployment is a manual step.\n• With Continuous Deployment, any updated working version of the application is automatically pushed to production.\n• Continuous Deployment mandates Continuous Delivery, but the opposite is not required."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxo6dwhrhc0","created":1670627934324,"text":"Steps"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxo6fb9fbs0","created":1670627937394,"text":"• With continuous delivery, every code change is built, tested, and then pushed to a non- production testing or staging environment.\n• There can be multiple, parallel test stages before a production deployment.\n• In the last step, the developer approves the update to production when they are ready.\n "},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxo5a2grfc0","created":1670627847614,"text":"• Automate"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxo4oqmzu80","created":1670627801186,"text":"\t • The build, deploy, test, and release process must be automated so that it is\nrepeatable."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxo5d4bdk00","created":1670627854256,"text":"• Frequent"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxo51r3hm00","created":1670627829512,"text":"• Releases must be frequent.\n• The delta between releases will be small.\n• This significantly reduces the risk associated with releasing and makes it much easier to roll back."},"children":[]}]}]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxo7jmd81c0","created":1670628025137,"text":"Cloud computing"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxo7wleeqw0","created":1670628053376,"text":"Types"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxo9qfwul40","created":1670628196712,"text":"On-Premise"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxob0km5hs0","created":1670628297128,"text":"Servers are acquired, operating systems are installed, other hardware may be involved, \nbut all of that lives within your four walls, or the walls of your datacenter."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxo9v51ze00","created":1670628206939,"text":"Scaling"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxobkvsii00","created":1670628341340,"text":"Scaling represents the ability of the resource to handle increased or decreased usage demands.\n• Horizontal Scaling – scaling out and scaling in 加机器\n• Vertical Scaling – scaling up and scaling down 胖机器"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxoe41qyy80","created":1670628539787,"text":"Business Drivers","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxoe9vq8f40","created":1670628552484,"text":"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t• Capacity Planning\n\t\t"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxokjgklnk0","created":1670629043520,"text":"the process of determining and fulfilling future demands of an organization's IT resources, products and services."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxoksxehhc0","created":1670629064129,"text":"• LeadStrategy–addingcapacitytoanITresourceinanticipationofdemand\n• LagStrategy–addingcapacitywhentheITresourcereachesitsfullcapacity\n• MatchStrategy–addingITresourcescapacityinsmallincrements,asdemandincreases"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxogke11aw0","created":1670628732087,"text":"• Cost Reduction"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxogme8nxc0","created":1670628736453,"text":"• Organizational Agility "},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxojm9esdc0","created":1670628971254,"text":"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tadapt and evolve to successfully face change caused by internal and external factors. \n\t\t"},"children":[]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxolbrolww0","created":1670629105142,"text":"Benefits of Cloud Computing","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxoljs0iq80","created":1670629122576,"text":"• Reduce upfront cost of hardware, software and ownership\n• Reduce or eliminate need for data center, cooling, backup datacenter & operations related cost\n• On-demand access to pay-as-you-go computing resources\n• Access to virtually unlimited computing resources\n• Access to different kind of computing resources\n• Flexibility in scaling resources almost instantaneously\n• Increased Availability & Reliability"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxolr5kchk0","created":1670629138633,"text":"Obstacles for Cloud Computing","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxolwx43ns0","created":1670629151183,"text":"• Reduced Control over operations.\n• Cloud Provider (Vendor) lock-in. Applications & Solutions may become\nvendor specific over time and make it difficult to migrate to different cloud\nprovider or to on-prem.\n• Reduce control over service configuration. Cloud services consumers can\nno longer fine tune parameters for services such as databases, etc.\n• Data Confidentiality & Auditability. Regional compliance and legal issues\ncan make it challenging to move application to cloud if the cloud provider\ndoes not meet the requirements. For e.g. HIPPA regulations.\n• Cloud consumers usually have no recourse when cloud platform suffers\noutage.\n• Performance Unpredictability\n• Software Licensing"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxom8m1w1c0","created":1670629176635,"text":"Cloud Characteristics"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxomgsczg00","created":1670629194431,"text":"1. On-demand self service\n2. Broad Network Access\n3. Resource Pooling (multitenancy)\n4. Elasticity: reduced investment and proportional cost benefit.\n5. Measured Service"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxonfi9luo0","created":1670629270008,"text":"Cloud Delivery Models","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxonwn6fxc0","created":1670629307310,"text":"Infrastructure-as-a-Service (IaaS)\n• IaaS provides self-contained IT environment consisting of hardware, networking, connectivity, Operating Systems and other “raw” it resources.\n• IaaS provides cloud consumers with a high level of control and responsibility over its configuration and utilization.\n• IaaS providers may offer customized physical hardware for servers, load balancers, Gateways, firewalls, Intrusion Detection System (IDS) and Intrusion Prevention Systems.\n• With IaaS, cloud consumer is responsible for securing the “raw” IT resources.\n• Popular IaaS providers are Amazon Web Services, Google Cloud Platform, Microsoft Azure, IBM SoftLayer, Rackspace"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxoo356nhc0","created":1670629321460,"text":"Platform as a Service (PaaS)\n• PaaS provides “ready-to-use” environment to deploy applications.\n• Cloud consumers do not have control over or manage the underlying cloud infrastructure such as network, servers, operating systems, storage, etc.\n• Popular PaaS providers are Heroku, IBM BlueMix and Google App Engine"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxoo9uww0w0","created":1670629336076,"text":"Software as a Service (SaaS)\n• A software program positioned as a shared cloud service and made available as a “product” or generic utility represents the typical profile of a SaaS offering.\n• Consumer of PaaS does not manage or control the underlying cloud infrastructure including network, servers, operating systems, storage, or even individual application capabilities, \nwith the possible exception of limited user specific application configuration settings.\n• Popular SaaS offering examples are Gmail, GitHub, Google Drive, etc."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxoovemuy80","created":1670629382981,"text":"Cloud Deployment Models"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxop4ry2sw0","created":1670629403377,"text":"1. Public cloud\n2. Community cloud\n3. Private cloud\n4. Hybrid cloud: two or more different cloud deployment models"},"children":[]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxo84j4fv40","created":1670628070653,"text":"Identity & Access Management (IAM)"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxoqduhwgw0","created":1670629501487,"text":"Identity and Access Management (IAM)\nthe security discipline that enables the right individuals to access the right resources at the right times for the right reasons."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxp2zpmuwo0","created":1670630489452,"text":"features"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxp44dz3a00","created":1670630577995,"text":"• Shared access to your AWS account\n• Granular permissions - You can grant different permissions to different people for different resources.\n• Secure access to AWS resources for applications that run on Amazon EC2 "},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxp5a7lxb40","created":1670630669035,"text":"Benefits","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxp6b0jzm00","created":1670630749149,"text":"• Enhanced Security\n• Granular control: highly detailed control\n• Temporary Credentials\n• Flexible security credential management: e.g. MFA, key pairs\n• Leverage external identity systems\n• Seamlessly integrated into AWS services"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxpcpdbvug0","created":1670631250582,"text":"IAM Best Practices"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxpci9tsjs0","created":1670631235132,"text":"• Lock Away Your AWS Account Root User Access Keys\n• Create Individual IAM Users\n• Use AWS Defined Policies to Assign Permissions Whenever Possible • Use Groups to Assign Permissions to IAM Users\n• Grant Least Privilege\n• Use Access Levels to Review IAM Permissions\n• Configure a Strong Password Policy for Your Users\n• Enable MFA for Privileged Users\n• Use Roles for Applications That Run on Amazon EC2 Instances\n• Delegate by Using Roles Instead of by Sharing Credentials\n• Rotate Credentials Regularly\n• Remove Unnecessary Credentials"},"children":[]}]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxpf29ep5k0","created":1670631435371,"text":"ComputerNetworking/\nInfrastructureasCode/\nAWS cloudformation"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxpg1d34fc0","created":1670631511781,"text":"Computer Networking"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxpjckhuh40","created":1670631771266,"text":"network"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxpttrfk9s0","created":1670632592332,"text":"Communication Link","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxpj4z2frs0","created":1670631754733,"text":"A network is simply bunch of systems connected to each other via some kind of communication link（coaxial, copper or fiber cable or radio waves）."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxpjib355k0","created":1670631783758,"text":"• The common type of networks are:\nØLAN: Local Area Network\nØWAN: Wide Area Network"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxps5dpfmg0","created":1670632460895,"text":" Data transmission rate of a link is measured in bits/second.\n• Note that we use bits/second and not bytes per second. 1 byte is 8 bits."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxpu0h2go00","created":1670632606943,"text":"Bandwidth"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxptyv84eo0","created":1670632603445,"text":"bandwidth is the maximum rate of data transfer across a given path"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxpuopgv8w0","created":1670632659694,"text":"Network Protocols"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxpuyls0eg0","created":1670632681238,"text":"Protocols define the format and order of messages exchanged between two or more communicating entities \nas well as the actions taken on transmission and/or receipt of a message or other event."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxpvdib44g0","created":1670632713680,"text":"Network Protocol Stack & OSI Reference Model","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxpvkqlvy80","created":1670632729420,"text":"Application Layer\nThis is the layer where network applications and application-layer protocols such as HTTP, FTP, etc. reside."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxpw3nx8iw0","created":1670632770616,"text":"Presentation & Session Layer\n• Session layer handles data related to particular user session.\n• Presentation layer handles rendering of data.\n• Example of presentation layer protocols are audio and video codes,\ndifferent image formats such as PNG, JPEG, etc."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxpwakymtk0","created":1670632785675,"text":"Transport Layer\n• The transport layer transports application layer messages between client and server side of the application.\n• Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) and User Datagram Protocol (UDP) are examples of some popular transport layer protocols."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxpwhebzuw0","created":1670632800511,"text":"Network Layer\n• The network layer is responsible for moving transport layer protocols from one host to another.\n• The network layer defines the fields in data packet (datagram) as well as how the end systems and routers act on this field.\n• If the data packet is too large, network layer might split it into multiple packets which will be combined into one at the receiving node.\n• Most popular network layer protocol is the Internet Protocol (IP)."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxpwn1petk0","created":1670632812808,"text":"Link Layer\nLink layer is responsible for moving packet from one node to the next node in the route."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxpws50o9s0","created":1670632823893,"text":"Physical Layer\nWhile the job of link layer is to move packets of data from one node to another, physical layer’s job is to move the individual bits."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxpy6dyl280","created":1670632933272,"text":"TCP"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxpyh0vglc0","created":1670632956426,"text":"Transmission Control Protocol (TCP)\n• Reliable Data Transfer - TCP ensures that data is delivered from sending process to receiving process correctly and in order.\n• TCP is connection based - Sender will keep connection with receiver open until it is done successfully sending all the data packets.\n• TCP does error checking and erroneous packets will be retransmitted from source to destination.\n• TCP has congestion control. It will not send next chunk of data until it has received OK (ACK) from the receiver.\n• Common application-layer protocol that use TCP/IP are HTTP, FTP, SMTP, etc."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxpyp759m80","created":1670632974219,"text":"UDP","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxpyrhickg0","created":1670632979199,"text":"User Datagram Protocol (UDP)\n• UDP protocol is designed to do as little work as possible to send data.\n• UDP has no congestion control. Data is transmitted as soon as it can.\n• UDP has no reliability guarantee. It does not care if the message was successfully received.\n• UDP has not overhead of connection establishment.\n• No connection state is maintained as we are not tracking\nsuccessful delivery of data or the order in which data is sent.\n• Common application-layer protocols that use UDP are Domain\nName Systems (DNS), Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP)"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxpz2741w00","created":1670633002515,"text":"IP"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxpz4lu3hk0","created":1670633007759,"text":"Internet Protocol (IP)\n• The Internet Protocol (IP) is the principal communications protocol in the Internet protocol suite for relaying datagrams across network boundaries.\n• IP is best-effort delivery service, makes no guarantee about delivering correct data, the order in which it is delivered or avoidance of delivering duplicate data pretty much like UDP.\n• First major version of IP is Internet Protocol Version 4 (IPv4) and it’s successor is Internet Protocol Version 6 (IPv6)."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxq0cde3ps0","created":1670633103027,"text":"Subnet/NAT","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxq0dw7c5s0","created":1670633106341,"text":"Subnet\n• A subnet is isolated network.\n• IP addresses are assigned to subnet\nwith a subnet mask.\n• Subnets are usually represented\nusing CIDR notation that is written as the first address of a network, followed by a slash character (/), and ending with the bit-length of the prefix."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxq142jwk00","created":1670633163322,"text":"Network Address Translation (NAT)\n• NAT allows sharing of one Internet-routable IP address of a NAT gateway for an entire private network."},"children":[]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxq1fxguyw0","created":1670633189135,"text":"Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)\t\t"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxq23xk5s00","created":1670633241384,"text":"defi"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxq1x3old40","created":1670633226517,"text":"• Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) allows you to launch resources into a virtual network you have defined."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxq26a4mts0","created":1670633246497,"text":"A VPC is a virtual network that belongs to you and is logically isolated from virtual networks that belong to other users."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxq2h7rq1s0","created":1670633270299,"text":"can create/config"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxq2mr6og00","created":1670633282357,"text":"• firewall\n• routing table\n• public or private subnets \n• network gateway"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxq2zirtww0","created":1670633310147,"text":"Elastic Network Interfaces"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxq3g2o5c00","created":1670633346178,"text":"a virtual network interface"},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxq3pl1tgo0","created":1670633366882,"text":"Route Tables"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxq3ywn2oo0","created":1670633387173,"text":"• A route table contains a set of rules, called routes, that are used to determine where network traffic is directed.\n• Each subnet in your VPC must be associated with a route table; the table controls the routing for the subnet.\n• A subnet can only be associated with one route table at a time, but you can associate multiple subnets with the same route table."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxq4h891co0","created":1670633427057,"text":"Internet Gateways"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxq4r17wvk0","created":1670633448399,"text":"defi: a horizontally scaled, redundant, and highly available VPC component that allows communication between instances in your VPC and the Internet."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxq4y13iu80","created":1670633463630,"text":"purposes:\n• to provide a target in your VPC route tables for Internet-routable traffic, and\n• to perform network address translation (NAT) for instances that have been assigned public IPv4 addresses."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"coxq5j13ork0","created":1670633509342,"text":"Elastic IP Addresses"},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"coxq5q9jg1k0","created":1670633525090,"text":"Security Groups"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"coxq5s9kpls0","created":1670633529446,"text":"• A security group acts as a virtual firewall for your instance to control inbound and outbound traffic."},"children":[]}]}]}]}]},{"data":{"id":"cp2q2sx7utc0","created":1671141095566,"text":"L12"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cp2q2xmrjq00","created":1671141105818,"text":"Building & Deploying Applications on Cloud","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cp2q3hh7te80","created":1671141149018,"text":"Lift & Shift\n• Migrating application to cloud without significant changes. \n• Gain benefits of a secure and cost-efficient infrastructure."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cp2q3p13q5k0","created":1671141165458,"text":"Maximize Cloud Investments\n• Make the most of the elasticity and agility that are possible with cloud computing, engineers have to evolve their architectures to take advantage of cloud capabilities.\n• For new applications, cloud-specific IT architecture patterns can help drive efficiency and scalability.\n• Whether you are rearchitecting the applications that currently run in your on-premises environment to run on AWS, \nor designing cloud- native applications, you must consider the differences between traditional environments and cloud computing environments."},"children":[]}]},{"data":{"id":"cp2q41bhn1s0","created":1671141192207,"text":"Differences Between Traditional and Cloud Computing Environments","expandState":"expand"},"children":[{"data":{"id":"cp2q4akx6ug0","created":1671141212369,"text":"On-Premises vs. Cloud\nCloud computing differs from a traditional, on-premises environment in many ways, including\n• Flexible\n• global and scalable capacity\n• managed services\n• built-in security\n• options for cost optimization\n• and various operating models."},"children":[]},{"data":{"id":"cp2q4jldapc0","created":1671141231987,"text":"IT Assets as Provisioned Resources\n• In a traditional computing environment, you provision capacity based on an estimate of a theoretical maximum peak. This can result in periods where expensive resources are sitting idle or occasions of insufficient capacity.\n• With cloud computing, you can access as much or as little capacity as you need and dynamically scale to meet actual demand, while only paying for what you use.\n• On cloud, servers, databases, storage, and higher-level application components can be instantiated within seconds.\n• You can treat these as temporary resources, free from the inflexibility and constraints of a fixed and finite IT infrastructure.\n• This resets the way you approach change management, testing, reliability, and capacity planning.\n• This change in approach encourages experimentation by introducing the ability in processes to fail fast and iterate quickly."},"children":[]}]}]}]},"template":"right","theme":"fresh-blue","version":"1.4.43"}